		{"id":"2bef3b58c19c780c","type":"text","text":"Voxel representation + 3D noise: makes it possible to obtain overhangs, tunnels, etc.","x":-7222,"y":2445,"width":431,"height":60},
		{"id":"ce4d15ce3eb3fdae","type":"text","text":"https://discussions.unity.com/t/digger-caves-overhangs-for-unity-terrains/726282","x":-7222,"y":2505,"width":431,"height":69},
		{"id":"9d2759597e4dabbb","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/3DNoiseVoxelDensityAttrib2.JPG","x":-7222,"y":1784,"width":400,"height":344},
		{"id":"25ff3b15882bacea","type":"text","text":"Octaves: number of noise layers","x":-6506,"y":2932,"width":300,"height":60},
		{"id":"a5e0352f54ff2e0c","type":"text","text":"Lacunarity: change in frequency for each noise layer (usually F2 = F1 * 2)","x":-6700,"y":3110,"width":250,"height":108},
		{"id":"00ed07eba5404f18","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinVoronoi1.JPG","x":-7713,"y":3279,"width":780,"height":307},
		{"id":"e076b631f02d8f7c","type":"text","text":"Persistence: change in amplitude for each noise level (usually A2 = A1 / 2)","x":-6356,"y":3110,"width":250,"height":108},
		{"id":"921760a540b46128","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinNoiseOctave0.JPG","x":-6706,"y":3278,"width":600,"height":307},
		{"id":"3dd292ed90478664","type":"text","text":"Perlin and Voronoi are structured noises and a function:\n\nv = noise(a)\n  \n  \n\"a\" is usually coordinate in space (3D position, UV coordinate, etc.)\n\n","x":-8267,"y":3279,"width":527,"height":240},
		{"id":"a2ba1f4d57712ce5","type":"text","text":"Noise domain warping\n\n* Concept: manipulate position value on which noise is evaluated and use the result to modify original position value\n* This way, effectively, space in which noise is computed is warped resulting in more complex shapes\n* https://iquilezles.org/articles/warp/","x":-5826,"y":4319,"width":695,"height":251,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"139da9bdd612861e","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinWarp0.JPG","x":-5807,"y":3278,"width":656,"height":311},
		{"id":"615658c3bba334ac","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinWarp1.JPG","x":-5807,"y":3618,"width":655,"height":303},
		{"id":"45d4cf51ff78ce0d","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinWarp2.JPG","x":-5807,"y":3953,"width":656,"height":326},
		{"id":"b5e099304ad87693","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/HeightfieldBased2.jpg","x":-10281,"y":2500,"width":400,"height":226},
		{"id":"2d3ec58cf0680e44","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinVoronoi3_Disp.JPG","x":-7713,"y":5117,"width":780,"height":360},
		{"id":"c56fafc76b99950d","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinVoronoi3.JPG","x":-7713,"y":4800,"width":780,"height":317},
		{"id":"6091befa23243651","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinVoronoi1_Disp.JPG","x":-7713,"y":3586,"width":780,"height":359},
		{"id":"2c63d58594999b8b","type":"text","text":"Fundamental parameter is amplitude, A:\n\nv = noise(f x a) x A","x":-8267,"y":4800,"width":527,"height":200},
		{"id":"dae778e190efbcac","type":"text","text":"Fundamental parameter is scale, that is frequency F:\n\nv = noise(F * a)","x":-8267,"y":4032,"width":527,"height":200},
		{"id":"30d22bb76b559883","type":"text","text":"Noise parameters: frequency and amplitude","x":-7740,"y":2799,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"721d0c956774371c","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinVoronoi2.JPG","x":-7713,"y":4032,"width":780,"height":347},
		{"id":"6799f3c6d25ddcec","type":"text","text":"Noise domain warping (distortion)","x":-5604,"y":2912,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"f1076410949ccdbe","type":"text","text":"Distorting evaluation space:\n* v = noise(distort(a) x F) x A","x":-5652,"y":3092,"width":347,"height":120},
		{"id":"ccc606842ecd3834","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/3DVoxelBasedInfo.jpg","x":-9772,"y":2753,"width":353,"height":400},
		{"id":"b57777280912b14f","type":"text","text":"https://iquilezles.org/articles/fbmsdf/","x":-9261,"y":2245,"width":493,"height":75},
		{"id":"76deebff1e62078d","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/SDF_FBM2.JPG","x":-9261,"y":1846,"width":493,"height":399},
		{"id":"43f2cc604ba761dd","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/HeightfieldBased1.jpg","x":-10281,"y":2227,"width":400,"height":226},
		{"id":"b34217893f6a2f57","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/3DVoxelBased2.jpg","x":-9772,"y":2493,"width":399,"height":226},
		{"id":"8b1b072abdc79091","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/3DVoxelBased1.jpg","x":-9772,"y":2226,"width":400,"height":227},
		{"id":"01f7ee03642c28ca","type":"text","text":"NB: modeling vs rendering. Heightmaps are efficient for modeling (e.g., flowfield operation) since calculations are performed on 2D grid. However, for rendering, Houdini converts heightmap to mesh which is the most efficient for rendering.","x":-10281,"y":1951,"width":350,"height":240},
		{"id":"5d747d8ecbe46b26","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/SDF_FBM.JPG","x":-9261,"y":1569,"width":493,"height":264},
		{"id":"b2ee889506a2d86e","type":"text","text":"NB: volume/voxel representation is in some cases more suitable tool for terrain modeling. But note that heightfield is converted to mesh for rendering and interactive application.","x":-9373,"y":2930,"width":300,"height":223},
		{"id":"24e00ddcd6627a15","type":"text","text":"NB: As opposed to Surface/Mesh and Heightfield/2D voxel grid, 3D voxel grid can represent overhangs, tunnels, etc.","x":-9772,"y":1973,"width":353,"height":195,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"c46b81bbede58adf","type":"text","text":"2D voxel grid/Surface\n\n> Represent terrain using 2D volumes where each voxel contains the height of the terrain at that grid point, called _height fields_","x":-10256,"y":1596,"width":300,"height":217,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"0d053701a60e56b6","type":"text","text":"3D voxel grid/Volume\n\n* Represent terrain using 3D  volumes where each voxel contains certain terrain information such as is it ground or empty space.","x":-9736,"y":1588,"width":280,"height":245,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"9922771c49448dd0","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/DataStructure_Geometry_Mesh.jpg","x":-10880,"y":1606,"width":238,"height":197},
		{"id":"16182b6f2df4c8d8","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/Torus.jpg","x":-10615,"y":1610,"width":251,"height":193},
		{"id":"6b186560eadbb4f6","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Tools/PerlinVoronoi2_Disp.JPG","x":-7713,"y":4379,"width":780,"height":349},
		{"id":"e318f80326026903","type":"text","text":"Layering multiple noises or height data:\n* Concept: layer noises of different scale and frequency\n\t* Larger scales define large details\n\t* Smaller scales define small details\n* FbM\n* Fractal sum","x":-6750,"y":4365,"width":700,"height":225},
		{"id":"c8976083988c3dc7","type":"file","file":"Images/Noise/Takeaways/base.jpg","x":-6706,"y":4638,"width":569,"height":320},
